---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import Evidence from '../../components/Evidence.astro';
import { essays } from '../../data/essays';
import { gh, u } from '../../lib/url';

const essay = essays.find((e) => e.slug === 'why-testkube')!;

const adr = (n: string, f: string) => gh('glidepath', `docs/admin/adr/${n}-${f}.md`);

// A sketch to show the shape, not a file in Hangar: four k6 workers against a Skyport service.
const sketch = `apiVersion: testworkflows.testkube.io/v1
kind: TestWorkflow
metadata:
  name: flight-api-load
spec:
  content:
    files:
      - path: /data/load.js
        content: |
          import http from 'k6/http';
          export default () => http.get('http://flight-api/healthz');
  steps:
    - name: Four workers at once
      parallel:
        count: 4
        transfer:
          - from: /data
        container:
          image: grafana/k6:latest
        shell: k6 run /data/load.js`;

const fit = [
  ['A resource, not a script', "A TestWorkflow has a schema, a status and an owner, like every other thing Hangar runs. It lives in the app repo and goes through review with the code it tests."],
  ['Any framework', "k6, Playwright, Cypress, JMeter, Postman, pytest. The paved road is where tests run and how results come back, not which tool a team has to learn."],
  ['Any caller', "Glidepath's Tekton pipeline starts it today, but the workflow doesn't know that. The same file runs from GitHub Actions, the CLI, a schedule or a Kubernetes event."],
];

const evidence = [
  { text: 'Testkube CE installed on every cluster with the CI/CD platform, through Apron and ArgoCD', status: 'built' as const, href: gh('apron', '50-platform-cicd/testkube/application.yaml') },
  { text: "Glidepath's test stage applies and runs an app's glidepath/<name>.yaml as a TestWorkflow", status: 'built' as const, href: gh('glidepath', 'docs/user/cicd-yaml-reference.md') },
  { text: 'One shared namespace, with secrets moved in for the run and blanked after (ADR-0007)', status: 'built' as const, href: adr('0007', 'testkube-shared-namespace') },
  { text: "A Kyverno policy so one app's tests can't mount another app's secrets (ADR-0008)", status: 'built' as const, href: adr('0008', 'kyverno-testkube-secret-policy') },
  { text: 'An outcome and one line per step, written for the Release Record in Tower', status: 'built' as const, href: gh('glidepath', 'charts/glidepath-catalog/templates/tasks/run-testworkflow.yaml') },
  { text: 'Parallel and sharded runs for the Skyport test suites', status: 'proposal' as const },
  { text: "Testkube's own CDEvents on the same event stream as Glidepath's", status: 'proposal' as const },
  { text: "Autopilot's Preflight evaluations running as TestWorkflows", status: 'proposal' as const, href: gh('autopilot', 'README.md') },
  { text: 'A control plane connection: per-namespace runs and one dashboard for every run', status: 'proposal' as const },
];
---
<Base title={essay.title} description={essay.dek} section="architecture">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="essay">
      <header class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a href={u('architecture/')}>Architecture</a> · {essay.minutes} min read</p>
        <h1>Why Hangar runs its tests in <em>Testkube</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">{essay.dek}</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Tests are where a lot of good pipelines quietly go wrong. Usually it isn't the tests themselves. It's where
          they end up living: a shell script at the bottom of a CI job, running one file at a time on one runner, with
          the results buried in a log that scrolls away before anyone reads it. The suite gets bigger, the wait gets
          longer, and the people who care most about quality have the least visibility into it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hangar runs its tests in <a href="https://testkube.io">Testkube</a>. This essay is about why: the proof of
          concept that sold me on it, how it works, and why it fits a platform built around an API-driven control
          plane. I'll also be honest about the free tier, because running it in my lab taught me a few things the docs
          didn't.
        </p>

        <h2>It started with the test engineers</h2>
        <p>
          In a previous role, I ran a Testkube proof of concept with our test engineers. They were good at their jobs
          and wanted to level up their tooling. Their suites were slow, and they had no way to run them in parallel, so
          a bigger suite just meant a longer wait. Their results were spread across pipeline runs, and what they asked
          for, in so many words, was a single pane of glass: every run, on every environment, in one place.
        </p>
        <p>
          The POC went really well. Parallelism was the thing they'd been missing, and seeing every result together
          was the thing they'd been asking for. What won me over, as the platform engineer in the room, was something
          they didn't care about at all: Testkube is Kubernetes-native. A test is a custom resource with a schema,
          sitting in the cluster beside everything else, and that's exactly the shape I want every part of a platform
          to have.
        </p>
        <p>
          The people helped too. The Testkube team was great to work with from start to finish. I came to them with a
          number of feature requests along the way, and they were well received, never lost in a support queue. When
          you're picking a tool you'll live with for years, how its makers treat a stranger's feature request tells you
          a lot.
        </p>

        <h2>How Testkube works</h2>
        <p>
          The core idea is that a test is a Kubernetes resource. You describe it in a <strong>TestWorkflow</strong>:
          which container image to run, the steps, any services it needs alongside it, like a database or a browser,
          and which files to keep as artifacts. Then something starts it: a CI pipeline, the CLI or API, a cron schedule
          declared in the workflow, or a Kubernetes event through a TestTrigger. The open source agent, running in your
          cluster, turns it into pods, runs it, and collects the logs, reports and per-step status.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="testing/01-how-testkube-works" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Two things make it much more than a fancy Job runner. The first is orchestration. A step can fan out into
          parallel workers, split a suite into shards, or run a matrix of configurations, then gather the results back
          up. That's the parallelization the test engineers were missing, and it's a few lines of YAML, not a rewrite.
          Here's the shape of it, four k6 workers against one of the Skyport services. It's a sketch to show the idea,
          not a file that lives in Hangar:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{sketch}</code></pre>
        <p>
          The second is that Testkube doesn't care which test framework you use. If it runs in a container, Testkube
          can orchestrate it. There's no framework lock-in, and that matters more than it sounds: test suites tend to
          outlive almost every tool around them, including the CI system that runs them.
        </p>
        <p>
          And you choose where it lives. The agent is open source and runs on its own. The control plane, where the
          dashboard and that single pane of glass live, comes as a hosted cloud service or on-premises, air-gapped if
          you need it. The flexibility runs the other way too: it plugs into GitHub Actions, GitLab, Jenkins, Argo CD,
          Tekton or plain kubectl, so the test never has to know which pipeline called it.
        </p>

        <h2>Why it fits Hangar</h2>
        <p>
          The third of my <a href={u('principles/')}>principles</a> is an API-driven control plane, ready for AI. In
          Hangar, everything is a declared resource that a controller reconciles, and every change is a reviewed
          commit. Testkube's model is that same shape, applied to testing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="split">{fit.map(([t, v]) => <div><dt>{t}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>)}</dl>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The control-plane fit matters more every month. When an agent asks whether a release is good, I want the
          answer to come from a typed resource with a status and a per-step result, not from somebody's regex over a
          log. A TestWorkflow and its executions give an agent the same thing Airframe's contract does: something it can
          read without guessing.
        </p>
        <p class="pull">Pipelines get rewritten. Test suites outlive them. Put the tests somewhere that doesn't care who called.</p>
        <p>
          It's event-friendly too, which is my fifth principle. Testkube can start a run when something changes in the
          cluster, and it can emit CDEvents, the same vocabulary Glidepath already uses for every stage of a release.
          Hangar doesn't wire Testkube's own events into that stream yet. It's on the list.
        </p>

        <h2>How a test runs in Hangar today</h2>
        <p>
          Testkube is built into <a href={u('cicd/')}>Glidepath</a>'s test stage. An app turns the stage on in its
          <code>cicd.yaml</code> and names a test. If the repo has a matching <code>glidepath/&lt;name&gt;.yaml</code>,
          that file is a TestWorkflow, written and owned by the app team, with no ticket and no operator involved.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="testing/02-a-test-run-in-hangar" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Glidepath's <code>run-testworkflow</code> step does the rest. It copies the app's secrets into Testkube's
          namespace for the length of the run, renames the workflow to <code>&lt;app&gt;-&lt;test&gt;</code> so it can't
          collide with another app's, applies it through a Kyverno policy gate, runs it with the Testkube CLI, and blanks
          the secret again afterwards. The outcome and one line per step become results of the pipeline run, which is
          what the Release Record in <a href={u('tower/')}>Tower</a> shows beside the build. Apps that haven't moved yet
          can still fall back to an <code>integration-test.sh</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          One thing I enjoyed finding: a TestWorkflow doesn't have to be a test. It's anything that runs and reports
          pass or fail. Warming a cache before a release fits just as well as a Playwright suite, so the step is named
          for what it does, not for what we assumed it would hold.
        </p>
        <p>
          And to be plain about what isn't there yet: Hangar doesn't use parallel or sharded runs today. The Skyport
          apps' suites are small enough that one worker is plenty. There's also no Testkube dashboard in my lab, so for
          now the single pane of glass is Tower's Release Record. The day the suites grow, parallelism is a change to a
          YAML file, not to the platform.
        </p>

        <h2>What the free tier taught me</h2>
        <p>
          Hangar runs Testkube's open source agent with no control plane, on purpose, because I wanted to know exactly
          what the free floor looks like. On August 24, 2026, I found out. The first design ran each app's tests in that
          app's own namespace, which the docs suggested was possible. The moment I applied it, the API server
          crash-looped. The answer was in the source: multi-namespace execution is gated to the paid editions, and the
          server exits if you ask for it in standalone mode.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="testing/03-what-the-free-tier-taught-me" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          So every app's tests run in one shared namespace, and that opened a real gap. Kubernetes RBAC decides who can
          edit a Secret, but not which Secret a pod is allowed to mount. Any tenant could have written another app's
          secret name into their own workflow. Testkube's per-run sensitive config is meant to solve exactly that, but
          it didn't work in the community build I was on. A Kyverno admission policy closed it instead, checking every
          TestWorkflow at the one moment the caller's real identity is still visible. Both halves of the story are
          written up, in <a href={adr('0007', 'testkube-shared-namespace')}>ADR-0007</a> and
          <a href={adr('0008', 'kyverno-testkube-secret-policy')}>ADR-0008</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          There were smaller lessons. My first "blank the secret" step didn't blank anything, because an empty object in
          a merge patch means "change nothing"; <code>data: null</code> is what actually clears it. Passing runs
          sometimes reported themselves as aborted, a race with the Job controller, so Glidepath now trusts the per-step
          results underneath. And the chart's default MongoDB wouldn't start on my nodes' kernel, so Testkube runs on its
          PostgreSQL backend instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of this changed my mind about Testkube. Open core means the paid features are paid, and I think that's a
          fair deal from a company that gives away a genuinely useful agent. I'd just rather know where the line is than
          guess. At a company, I'd buy the control plane without much debate: the single pane of glass is what the test
          engineers asked for, and it's what made the POC land. In a home lab where I'm the only test engineer, the free
          agent plus one well-placed policy is the right size.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's consistent with what I wrote in <a href={u('architecture/cost-of-the-platform/')}>What Hangar
          costs</a>, where Testkube is on the list of things a small team could leave out at first. I still believe
          that. Run the tests in the pipeline until someone asks for more. In my experience the someone is the test
          engineers, they ask for parallel runs and one place to look, and they ask early.
        </p>

        <h2>Where I'd take it next</h2>
        <p>
          Hangar's support for testing is likely to grow, and Testkube is the integration point I'd build it on:
          whatever kind of testing comes next, it arrives as another TestWorkflow rather than another bespoke pipeline
          step. Three things are on my mind, none of them built yet. Testkube's own CDEvents should join the event stream Glidepath already
          writes, so a test run shows up in the same trace as the build that triggered it. The Skyport suites should get
          big enough to earn sharding. And the one I'm most curious about: Autopilot's Preflight cases, which score how
          an agent behaves, are tests in every way that matters. Running them as TestWorkflows would give agent
          evaluations the same home, the same history and the same policy fence as everything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Testing is the part of the lifecycle where platforms most often give up and hand teams a script. Testkube let
          me treat it like the rest of Hangar: declared, reviewed, run by a controller, and readable by people and
          machines alike. And the team behind it made a stranger with a list of feature requests feel welcome, which is
          a good sign of how they'll treat the next one.
        </p>
        <p class="sign">James</p>
        <p class="more"><a href={u('architecture/')}>← All architecture essays</a></p>
      </div>

      <Evidence items={evidence} heading="Where Hangar does this, and where it doesn't yet" />
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .essay .hero .eyebrow a { color: inherit; }
  .essay .hero h1 { max-width: 22ch; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .prose pre {
    margin: 1.4rem 0; padding: 0.9rem 1rem; overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--raised); font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .prose pre code { background: none; padding: 0; font-size: inherit; }
  .pull {
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.4vw + 1rem, 2rem); line-height: 1.25;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1.2rem; margin: 2.5rem 0; max-width: 34ch;
  }
  .split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.75rem; margin: 1.2rem 0 1rem; max-width: 60rem; }
  @media (min-width: 800px) { .split { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr; } }
  .split div { background: var(--raised); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 8px; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; }
  .split dt { font-weight: 600; }
  .split dd { margin: 0.3rem 0 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .sign { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.4rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .more { margin-top: 2rem; }
</style>
